WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:00.763
Welcome.

2
00:00:00.763 --> 00:00:04.248
This is the technical deep dive
for a companion guide in the

3
00:00:04.248 --> 00:00:08.587
Federal Modernization Workbook:
Ansible for Configuration Automation.

4
00:00:08.587 --> 00:00:10.974
It's for the engineers
who decide which tool

5
00:00:10.974 --> 00:00:13.625
may change which setting,
and prove it afterward.

6
00:00:13.625 --> 00:00:16.187
The guide's idea fits in one line.

7
00:00:16.187 --> 00:00:20.521
Ansible can reach almost anything
with an S S H port or an A P I,

8
00:00:20.521 --> 00:00:25.738
so a program has to decide, setting by
setting, where it's allowed to write.

9
00:00:25.738 --> 00:00:28.963
Anything the guide couldn't
confirm stays labeled that way.

10
00:00:28.963 --> 00:00:31.140
Let's start with why this matters.

11
00:00:34.890 --> 00:00:35.928
Here's the problem.

12
00:00:35.928 --> 00:00:39.127
Ansible has no agent and
no record of its own.

13
00:00:39.127 --> 00:00:42.602
It connects, does what the
playbook says, and leaves.

14
00:00:42.602 --> 00:00:46.640
That makes it wonderfully flexible,
and it makes collisions invisible.

15
00:00:46.640 --> 00:00:49.846
Point it at a server that
Azure machine configuration

16
00:00:49.846 --> 00:00:52.340
already governs, and neither tool notices.

17
00:00:52.340 --> 00:00:55.893
Turn on auto-correct, and the
setting quietly flips back,

18
00:00:55.893 --> 00:00:57.515
with no report that it did.

19
00:00:57.515 --> 00:01:02.065
Two tools can fight over one value
forever while both report success.

20
00:01:02.065 --> 00:01:04.047
The guide's answer is simple:

21
00:01:04.047 --> 00:01:07.753
one owning tool per setting,
written down in the C M D B.

22
00:01:07.753 --> 00:01:10.400
First, what Ansible actually is.

23
00:01:14.150 --> 00:01:17.962
This first module places Ansible
among the tools you already run.

24
00:01:17.962 --> 00:01:21.541
We'll settle what it is and what
it isn't, set it beside Intune,

25
00:01:21.541 --> 00:01:26.312
Arc and Configuration Manager, and lay
down the rule that stops them fighting.

26
00:01:26.312 --> 00:01:29.650
Then we'll follow it into the
places those tools can't reach,

27
00:01:29.650 --> 00:01:32.380
starting with onboarding servers to Arc.

28
00:01:36.130 --> 00:01:39.043
It helps to be precise about
what kind of tool this is.

29
00:01:39.043 --> 00:01:40.304
Ansible pushes.

30
00:01:40.304 --> 00:01:43.495
It connects over S S
H or Windows remoting,

31
00:01:43.495 --> 00:01:46.967
runs small modules, removes
them, and it's gone.

32
00:01:46.967 --> 00:01:52.504
You describe the work in Y A M L
playbooks, against an inventory of hosts.

33
00:01:52.504 --> 00:01:56.039
The free engine does the work,
and the Automation Platform

34
00:01:56.039 --> 00:01:59.867
wraps it with a controller, a
content hub and packaged runtimes.

35
00:01:59.867 --> 00:02:01.793
What it isn't is a management plane.

36
00:02:01.793 --> 00:02:06.882
And while tasks should be safe to
repeat, the documentation admits not all

37
00:02:06.882 --> 00:02:11.554
of them are, which is why check mode,
a true dry run, matters so much here.

38
00:02:11.554 --> 00:02:13.990
So how does it sit beside the others?

39
00:02:17.740 --> 00:02:21.327
Every population in the estate
already has a natural owner.

40
00:02:21.327 --> 00:02:25.690
Intune owns end-user devices, and
it doesn't cover Windows Server.

41
00:02:25.690 --> 00:02:29.640
Arc with machine configuration
owns servers outside Azure,

42
00:02:29.640 --> 00:02:31.615
on Microsoft's supported list.

43
00:02:31.615 --> 00:02:35.215
Azure Policy governs the
Azure resources themselves.

44
00:02:35.215 --> 00:02:39.790
Configuration Manager stays where it's
already deployed, with no new use.

45
00:02:39.790 --> 00:02:42.178
Ansible's row is the interesting one.

46
00:02:42.178 --> 00:02:45.816
It can reach anything, but
it owns only the tier two

47
00:02:45.816 --> 00:02:49.366
assets the C M D B names it
for, plus Arc onboarding.

48
00:02:49.366 --> 00:02:51.746
Owning is the key word,
so let's look at what

49
00:02:51.746 --> 00:02:55.190
happens when two tools both
think they own something.

50
00:02:58.940 --> 00:03:00.603
This is the heart of the guide.

51
00:03:00.603 --> 00:03:04.820
When Ansible and machine
configuration both set the same value,

52
00:03:04.820 --> 00:03:06.889
nothing in either tool notices.

53
00:03:06.889 --> 00:03:10.983
Microsoft's own caution is narrow,
but the principle is general:

54
00:03:10.983 --> 00:03:13.265
don't manage the same setting twice.

55
00:03:13.265 --> 00:03:18.165
So the program's rule, which is design
guidance, turns ownership into data.

56
00:03:18.165 --> 00:03:22.365
Each C M D B record names
one configuration owner.

57
00:03:22.365 --> 00:03:25.983
Ansible can read anything, but
it writes only where it owns,

58
00:03:25.983 --> 00:03:30.128
and that's enforced in the inventory
and again inside the playbook.

59
00:03:30.128 --> 00:03:32.628
Onboarding a server to
Arc isn't owning it.

60
00:03:32.628 --> 00:03:35.015
And any exception is a dated record.

61
00:03:35.015 --> 00:03:38.500
Now, where does Ansible sit
in the bigger structure?

62
00:03:42.250 --> 00:03:45.626
The workbook sorts every
asset into coverage tiers,

63
00:03:45.626 --> 00:03:48.138
and Ansible's job changes with the tier.

64
00:03:48.138 --> 00:03:52.038
In tier one it onboards and reads,
but it doesn't own settings.

65
00:03:52.038 --> 00:03:57.667
Tier two is its natural home: it owns
configuration where the C M D B says so,

66
00:03:57.667 --> 00:04:00.763
and every job's result becomes evidence.

67
00:04:00.763 --> 00:04:05.012
In tier three it only reads, and in
tier four it does nothing at all.

68
00:04:05.012 --> 00:04:08.512
Of the workbook's four
tracks, Ansible lives in two.

69
00:04:08.512 --> 00:04:10.574
The Arc track, for servers.

70
00:04:10.574 --> 00:04:14.194
And the non-person identity
track, because the controller

71
00:04:14.194 --> 00:04:17.737
itself is an identity with
standing access to many hosts.

72
00:04:17.737 --> 00:04:19.800
Let's start with the Arc job.

73
00:04:23.550 --> 00:04:27.065
Microsoft lists Ansible as one
of the ways to do it at scale,

74
00:04:27.065 --> 00:04:30.800
with a supported role that skips
machines already connected.

75
00:04:30.800 --> 00:04:32.913
The real choice is how it signs in,

76
00:04:32.913 --> 00:04:36.200
because that decides how
many secrets you inherit.

77
00:04:36.200 --> 00:04:40.115
A managed identity is Microsoft's
recommendation for production,

78
00:04:40.115 --> 00:04:42.250
since there's no stored secret at all.

79
00:04:42.250 --> 00:04:46.625
Next best is a certificate service
principal, brokered by the platform.

80
00:04:46.625 --> 00:04:49.275
Command-line sign-in is for pilots only.

81
00:04:49.275 --> 00:04:54.075
Whatever signs in gets the onboarding role
on one resource group, and nothing more.

82
00:04:54.075 --> 00:04:55.670
Then come the waves.

83
00:04:59.420 --> 00:05:01.595
This is where Ansible earns its keep.

84
00:05:01.595 --> 00:05:04.282
Because the inventory
comes from the C M D B,

85
00:05:04.282 --> 00:05:07.583
the onboarding run can stamp
the owner, environment,

86
00:05:07.583 --> 00:05:11.805
location and patch group onto each
Arc resource in the same change,

87
00:05:11.805 --> 00:05:15.183
so the cloud's view starts
out traceable to its source.

88
00:05:15.183 --> 00:05:20.370
The work runs in waves, and each wave's
credential goes away when the wave closes.

89
00:05:20.370 --> 00:05:25.419
If a playbook calls the agent directly,
the secret goes in a configuration file,

90
00:05:25.419 --> 00:05:28.682
never on the command line,
so it stays out of the logs.

91
00:05:28.682 --> 00:05:32.082
The example playbook shows this
behind a confirmation gate.

92
00:05:32.082 --> 00:05:34.970
Next, the hosts Arc can't take at all.

93
00:05:38.720 --> 00:05:44.023
A good share of a federal estate sits
outside Arc's support list: the Unix systems,

94
00:05:44.023 --> 00:05:46.835
and several older Linux releases that got

95
00:05:46.835 --> 00:05:49.808
their last new agent
version in October 2026.

96
00:05:49.808 --> 00:05:54.145
Those are tier two, and Ansible
reaches them over S S H.

97
00:05:54.145 --> 00:05:55.308
Two cautions.

98
00:05:55.308 --> 00:05:58.832
Privilege escalation can't be
narrowed to specific commands,

99
00:05:58.832 --> 00:06:03.532
so that account has broad rights and has
to be governed like a privileged identity.

100
00:06:03.532 --> 00:06:08.471
And check mode is only a simulation, so
each drift check should stand on its own.

101
00:06:08.471 --> 00:06:10.807
Network gear is the easy win.

102
00:06:10.807 --> 00:06:13.388
A read-only facts run gives you the model,

103
00:06:13.388 --> 00:06:17.220
serial and version of every
device, with nothing installed.

104
00:06:17.220 --> 00:06:21.190
Where a vendor controller exists,
it owns the configuration.

105
00:06:24.940 --> 00:06:27.874
The remaining targets
all follow one pattern:

106
00:06:27.874 --> 00:06:32.815
find the tool that already owns the setting,
and let Ansible do only what's left.

107
00:06:32.815 --> 00:06:35.779
On VMware, the virtual
machines can go to Arc,

108
00:06:35.779 --> 00:06:40.802
while the hypervisor and appliances,
which can't run an agent, are Ansible's.

109
00:06:40.802 --> 00:06:43.806
On Infoblox, Ansible
works through the A P I,

110
00:06:43.806 --> 00:06:47.240
with an account scoped to
the objects it manages.

111
00:06:47.240 --> 00:06:50.849
On a Windows server under Arc,
the operating system belongs

112
00:06:50.849 --> 00:06:54.678
to machine configuration, but
the application can be Ansible's.

113
00:06:54.678 --> 00:06:57.902
And in the cloud, the
provider's inventory is for

114
00:06:57.902 --> 00:07:00.878
checking against the C
M D B, not replacing it.

115
00:07:00.878 --> 00:07:03.090
Next, running the platform.

116
00:07:06.840 --> 00:07:09.523
The second module is about
running the Automation

117
00:07:09.523 --> 00:07:12.590
Platform somewhere a federal
assessor will look closely.

118
00:07:12.590 --> 00:07:18.315
The key fact is that, in almost every
option, it's software the agency runs itself.

119
00:07:18.315 --> 00:07:20.855
We'll cover what's
authorized and what isn't,

120
00:07:20.855 --> 00:07:24.410
the cryptography and hardening
questions, where to host it,

121
00:07:24.410 --> 00:07:29.480
the controls to switch on, and why the
controller is an identity in its own right.

122
00:07:33.230 --> 00:07:34.905
Start with what you're authorizing.

123
00:07:34.905 --> 00:07:40.148
The current release has full support
into 2027, with maintenance after that,

124
00:07:40.148 --> 00:07:43.643
and it installs only as
containers or on OpenShift.

125
00:07:43.643 --> 00:07:46.368
On authorization, be careful with words.

126
00:07:46.368 --> 00:07:49.144
The self-managed platform
isn't a cloud service,

127
00:07:49.144 --> 00:07:53.843
so it isn't on the FedRAMP Marketplace,
and nobody publishes a statement about it.

128
00:07:53.843 --> 00:07:56.418
Neither managed offering is listed either,

129
00:07:56.418 --> 00:07:59.493
and Azure Government
availability isn't published.

130
00:07:59.493 --> 00:08:03.740
So the program's default, which is
design guidance, is to run it yourself,

131
00:08:03.740 --> 00:08:07.842
inside your own boundary, and
authorize it like any other component.

132
00:08:07.842 --> 00:08:11.230
Then come the two questions
every assessor asks.

133
00:08:14.980 --> 00:08:18.042
F I P S and baseline
content come up first,

134
00:08:18.042 --> 00:08:21.105
and on F I P S the honest
answer is partial.

135
00:08:21.105 --> 00:08:26.477
Red Hat's F I P S certificates cover the
operating system, but the current platform

136
00:08:26.477 --> 00:08:31.367
documentation doesn't say whether the
platform is supported in F I P S mode.

137
00:08:31.367 --> 00:08:36.068
So treat that as unverified: ask
Red Hat in writing, and test it.

138
00:08:36.068 --> 00:08:40.881
Where your agency needs validated
cryptography, the controller's host

139
00:08:40.881 --> 00:08:44.879
runs in F I P S mode, so that
support is what you have to confirm.

140
00:08:44.879 --> 00:08:47.509
For the baseline, start from the C I S

141
00:08:47.509 --> 00:08:50.955
Benchmarks and your agency's
own C M 6 settings.

142
00:08:50.955 --> 00:08:55.532
Red Hat ships C I S profiles with
an Ansible playbook to apply them,

143
00:08:55.532 --> 00:09:00.193
and community C I S roles exist too,
as long as you review and pin them.

144
00:09:00.193 --> 00:09:03.381
The controller's host is
hardened like any other server.

145
00:09:03.381 --> 00:09:08.867
A S T I G for the controller exists, but
that's High only, an optional reference.

146
00:09:08.867 --> 00:09:10.500
Next, hosting.

147
00:09:14.250 --> 00:09:17.200
Where the controller runs
decides what it inherits.

148
00:09:17.200 --> 00:09:20.062
On premises puts it
closest to the targets.

149
00:09:20.062 --> 00:09:24.618
Self-managed on cloud virtual machines
inherits that cloud's authorization,

150
00:09:24.618 --> 00:09:27.466
checked service by service
and region by region,

151
00:09:27.466 --> 00:09:31.025
and on an Azure virtual machine
it can use a managed identity.

152
00:09:31.025 --> 00:09:34.394
The two managed offerings aren't
FedRAMP-listed, and that,

153
00:09:34.394 --> 00:09:38.337
not their commercial regions, is
what keeps them out of the default.

154
00:09:38.337 --> 00:09:41.289
One of them also erases
job logs after thirty days,

155
00:09:41.289 --> 00:09:44.312
so you'd have to export them
to keep your audit trail.

156
00:09:44.312 --> 00:09:48.495
And wherever it runs, the traffic
to its targets crosses a boundary,

157
00:09:48.495 --> 00:09:51.613
so record each channel as
an external connection.

158
00:09:51.613 --> 00:09:54.050
Now, the settings to switch on.

159
00:09:57.800 --> 00:10:01.662
Three groups of settings decide
whether the controller is auditable.

160
00:10:01.662 --> 00:10:03.026
First, sign-in.

161
00:10:03.026 --> 00:10:06.961
Federate it to your identity provider,
so your conditional access and

162
00:10:06.961 --> 00:10:11.100
phishing-resistant sign-in apply, and
keep local accounts for break-glass.

163
00:10:11.100 --> 00:10:14.070
The default lifetime used
to be a thousand years,

164
00:10:14.070 --> 00:10:17.337
and old tokens keep it, so
revoke them when you upgrade.

165
00:10:17.337 --> 00:10:18.762
Second, logging.

166
00:10:18.762 --> 00:10:23.450
Send everything to your SIEM, and set
the cleanup jobs to match your retention.

167
00:10:23.450 --> 00:10:25.000
Third, content.

168
00:10:25.000 --> 00:10:27.900
Hub signing is off by
default, so turn it on.

169
00:10:27.900 --> 00:10:30.225
And project signing is the quiet hero,

170
00:10:30.225 --> 00:10:33.450
because a project that fails
its signature won't run.

171
00:10:33.450 --> 00:10:36.787
That links the reviewed change
to what actually executes.

172
00:10:36.787 --> 00:10:39.470
Which brings us to the controller itself.

173
00:10:43.220 --> 00:10:47.507
Think about what a controller holds:
the keys to every host it manages.

174
00:10:47.507 --> 00:10:51.909
That's more reach than almost any
administrator, so the program treats

175
00:10:51.909 --> 00:10:56.083
it as a privileged non-person
identity, and this is design guidance.

176
00:10:56.083 --> 00:10:58.294
It gets two named human owners.

177
00:10:58.294 --> 00:11:02.582
Each credential does one job,
in one scope, and expires.

178
00:11:02.582 --> 00:11:06.618
Where you can avoid it, no secret
is stored in the controller at all:

179
00:11:06.618 --> 00:11:09.607
use a managed identity
first, then certificates,

180
00:11:09.607 --> 00:11:12.745
and fetch anything left from
your vault at run time.

181
00:11:12.745 --> 00:11:16.633
Launching a job that changes
production is a privileged action,

182
00:11:16.633 --> 00:11:19.420
logged to a person and
reviewed every quarter.

183
00:11:19.420 --> 00:11:21.010
That's the platform.

184
00:11:24.760 --> 00:11:26.822
The third module is about proof.

185
00:11:26.822 --> 00:11:31.963
Used properly, Ansible produces evidence
as a side effect: the playbook is a

186
00:11:31.963 --> 00:11:36.772
file in Git, the change is a pull request,
and the run is a job with a result.

187
00:11:36.772 --> 00:11:40.797
We'll map that to the controls assessors
test, show where the results land,

188
00:11:40.797 --> 00:11:43.900
and then walk through the
seven ways it goes wrong.

189
00:11:47.650 --> 00:11:51.088
Assessors want five things
from configuration management,

190
00:11:51.088 --> 00:11:53.838
and Ansible can show each
one without extra work.

191
00:11:53.838 --> 00:11:58.187
The baseline is simply the playbook
version, by Git commit and tag.

192
00:11:58.187 --> 00:12:01.863
Change control is the pull
request with a required reviewer.

193
00:12:01.863 --> 00:12:06.284
The same playbook that applies settings
can run in check mode to report drift,

194
00:12:06.284 --> 00:12:09.462
so implementation and monitoring
come from one source.

195
00:12:09.462 --> 00:12:13.038
Removing packages you don't
need shows least functionality.

196
00:12:13.038 --> 00:12:17.962
And because the inventory comes from the
C M D B, Ansible never invents a host,

197
00:12:17.962 --> 00:12:22.251
and anything it couldn't reach says
so instead of passing quietly.

198
00:12:22.251 --> 00:12:24.640
Here's where all that evidence goes.

199
00:12:28.390 --> 00:12:32.440
The rest of the evidence covers audit,
privilege and the supply chain.

200
00:12:32.440 --> 00:12:35.665
Job records and the activity
stream go to the SIEM.

201
00:12:35.665 --> 00:12:39.628
Least privilege comes from the
per-purpose credentials we just covered,

202
00:12:39.628 --> 00:12:43.327
and supply chain evidence comes
from pinned, verified collections.

203
00:12:43.327 --> 00:12:44.703
Then comes the payoff.

204
00:12:44.703 --> 00:12:49.775
Every job result goes to the program's
evidence store in the same shape every other

205
00:12:49.775 --> 00:12:53.878
tool uses, and because the inventory
came from the C M D B, each result

206
00:12:53.878 --> 00:12:58.503
already carries the configuration item
number that joins it all together.

207
00:12:58.503 --> 00:13:00.420
Now, the dangers.

208
00:13:04.170 --> 00:13:07.257
Most Ansible disasters
come from three failures.

209
00:13:07.257 --> 00:13:09.044
The first is blast radius,

210
00:13:09.044 --> 00:13:13.295
where one playbook aimed too
wide changes every host at once.

211
00:13:13.295 --> 00:13:17.947
So run in check mode first, target
narrowly, and change in small batches,

212
00:13:17.947 --> 00:13:21.957
where the first batch is a canary
and a failure stops the rest.

213
00:13:21.957 --> 00:13:27.657
The second is inventory drift, where a
hand-kept host list disagrees with reality.

214
00:13:27.657 --> 00:13:31.334
Generate the inventory from
the C M D B on every run,

215
00:13:31.334 --> 00:13:34.383
and fail on a bad record
rather than guess.

216
00:13:34.383 --> 00:13:36.357
The third is credential sprawl.

217
00:13:36.357 --> 00:13:40.507
Keep credentials in one controller,
and keep secrets out of the output.

218
00:13:40.507 --> 00:13:45.183
A strict lint profile on every pull
request makes much of this automatic.

219
00:13:45.183 --> 00:13:46.540
Four more to go.

220
00:13:50.290 --> 00:13:51.627
These four are quieter.

221
00:13:51.627 --> 00:13:55.941
Two tools on one setting flip it back
and forth while both report success,

222
00:13:55.941 --> 00:14:00.327
and the fix is the ownership rule,
checked inside every changing playbook.

223
00:14:00.327 --> 00:14:03.707
Public Galaxy content can
change under you between runs,

224
00:14:03.707 --> 00:14:07.446
so pull collections from a reviewed
source, pin exact versions,

225
00:14:07.446 --> 00:14:11.328
verify them, and keep that list as
your software bill of materials.

226
00:14:11.328 --> 00:14:15.107
Running everything as root gives
every mistake root's reach,

227
00:14:15.107 --> 00:14:17.677
so escalate only on
the tasks that need it.

228
00:14:17.677 --> 00:14:22.540
And commands that report a change every
time turn drift reports into noise.

229
00:14:22.540 --> 00:14:27.140
Run each playbook twice in test, and
the second run should change nothing.

230
00:14:27.140 --> 00:14:29.760
Now, how to adopt all of this.

231
00:14:33.510 --> 00:14:36.610
The last module turns
all of this into a path.

232
00:14:36.610 --> 00:14:41.948
It starts read-only and earns its way to
enforcement, with a gate at each step.

233
00:14:41.948 --> 00:14:46.328
Then we'll open the example folder:
the inventory every playbook reads,

234
00:14:46.328 --> 00:14:49.046
the four playbooks and
what each one proves,

235
00:14:49.046 --> 00:14:53.340
how they're tested, and finally
the checklist and the decisions.

236
00:14:57.090 --> 00:15:00.580
The adoption path is design
guidance, and its point is that

237
00:15:00.580 --> 00:15:04.215
nothing gets write access until
the read-only evidence is in.

238
00:15:04.215 --> 00:15:09.001
Phase zero is just facts, from an
inventory built off the C M D B.

239
00:15:09.001 --> 00:15:12.352
Phase one onboards
servers to Arc in waves.

240
00:15:12.352 --> 00:15:15.873
Phase two writes baselines
for the tier two platforms,

241
00:15:15.873 --> 00:15:19.002
but runs them in check mode
only, reporting drift.

242
00:15:19.002 --> 00:15:23.620
Only in phase three does anything
get enforced, by pull request,

243
00:15:23.620 --> 00:15:28.327
canary first, on hosts Ansible
owns, and only after two clean runs.

244
00:15:28.327 --> 00:15:34.341
And phase zero can start this week, with
a small control node and a C M D B export.

245
00:15:34.341 --> 00:15:36.340
Now, that inventory.

246
00:15:40.090 --> 00:15:43.646
Every pattern in the examples
depends on one file:

247
00:15:43.646 --> 00:15:47.115
the inventory script that
reads the C M D B export.

248
00:15:47.115 --> 00:15:52.512
It turns each record's attributes into
groups, by tier, by platform, by environment,

249
00:15:52.512 --> 00:15:55.573
and most importantly by
configuration owner,

250
00:15:55.573 --> 00:15:59.440
so the baseline check can only
ever see hosts Ansible owns.

251
00:15:59.440 --> 00:16:04.915
A server with no owner isn't a target at
all; it's a finding to fix in the C M D B.

252
00:16:04.915 --> 00:16:07.856
And if the export has a
duplicate or a missing column,

253
00:16:07.856 --> 00:16:11.140
the script stops rather than
hand you a partial inventory.

254
00:16:11.140 --> 00:16:14.477
In production, you'd use an
inventory plugin instead.

255
00:16:14.477 --> 00:16:16.680
Now, the playbooks themselves.

256
00:16:20.430 --> 00:16:25.068
There are four small playbooks, and each
one proves a single idea from the guide.

257
00:16:25.068 --> 00:16:29.397
The first is the inventory feed:
what each server actually is,

258
00:16:29.397 --> 00:16:34.506
beside what the C M D B says, with
unreached hosts marked as not collected.

259
00:16:34.506 --> 00:16:38.518
The second is the only one that
changes anything, and it's gated.

260
00:16:38.518 --> 00:16:43.045
Without an explicit confirmation it
only simulates, and when it does run,

261
00:16:43.045 --> 00:16:46.517
it starts with one canary and
stops at the first failure.

262
00:16:46.517 --> 00:16:49.679
The third is a drift check
locked into check mode,

263
00:16:49.679 --> 00:16:52.129
so a change result simply means drift.

264
00:16:52.129 --> 00:16:56.130
And the fourth reads network and
Infoblox facts without any agent.

265
00:16:56.130 --> 00:16:58.170
Next, how they're tested.

266
00:17:01.920 --> 00:17:04.245
The tests matter as much as the playbooks.

267
00:17:04.245 --> 00:17:08.532
Everything passes the strictest
lint profile and a syntax check.

268
00:17:08.532 --> 00:17:11.425
A read-only checker fails
on any ungated write,

269
00:17:11.425 --> 00:17:14.470
and its own negative tests
prove it catches them.

270
00:17:14.470 --> 00:17:17.595
The inventory tests prove
that bad records fail.

271
00:17:17.595 --> 00:17:19.370
But be clear about the limits.

272
00:17:19.370 --> 00:17:21.808
The tests ran on a control node only.

273
00:17:21.808 --> 00:17:25.372
The Arc, Windows, Cisco and
Infoblox paths pass lint,

274
00:17:25.372 --> 00:17:28.258
but they haven't touched those platforms.

275
00:17:28.258 --> 00:17:33.669
And the baseline is a handful of example
settings, not a C I S Benchmark profile.

276
00:17:33.669 --> 00:17:35.557
Treat them as illustrations.

277
00:17:35.557 --> 00:17:37.570
Next, the checklist.

278
00:17:41.320 --> 00:17:43.998
If you take one thing
away, take the checklist,

279
00:17:43.998 --> 00:17:47.545
because it turns the whole guide
into things you can verify.

280
00:17:47.545 --> 00:17:51.246
It runs across eight areas,
from ownership and inventory,

281
00:17:51.246 --> 00:17:56.207
through change safety and credentials,
to evidence and the platform itself.

282
00:17:56.207 --> 00:17:58.337
Each item is phrased as done when,

283
00:17:58.337 --> 00:18:01.570
so it works as a review gate
rather than a reminder.

284
00:18:01.570 --> 00:18:03.095
You've seen most of it today.

285
00:18:03.095 --> 00:18:08.039
The items teams skip are the quiet ones:
requiring a second run with no changes,

286
00:18:08.039 --> 00:18:11.283
and never counting an
unreached host as compliant.

287
00:18:11.283 --> 00:18:13.440
Let's finish with the decisions.

288
00:18:17.190 --> 00:18:21.477
Every decision here has one owner,
and this is design guidance.

289
00:18:21.477 --> 00:18:24.578
The C M D B owner, working
with the platform teams,

290
00:18:24.578 --> 00:18:28.553
decides the attributes and the
configuration owner values.

291
00:18:28.553 --> 00:18:32.827
Server infrastructure picks the
onboarding identity and the waves.

292
00:18:32.827 --> 00:18:36.865
The CISO's configuration authority
picks the baseline content.

293
00:18:36.865 --> 00:18:41.527
Platform owners decide when enforcement
goes live, through change control.

294
00:18:41.527 --> 00:18:44.781
And the system owner and the
authorizing official decide

295
00:18:44.781 --> 00:18:47.764
where the platform runs and
how it holds credentials.

296
00:18:47.764 --> 00:18:50.855
You can start this week:
add the owner attribute,

297
00:18:50.855 --> 00:18:53.390
build groups from it, and run facts only.

298
00:18:53.390 --> 00:18:55.652
Ansible can reach almost anything,

299
00:18:55.652 --> 00:18:59.240
so decide where it may write,
and let the evidence follow.

300
00:18:59.240 --> 00:19:00.050
Thank you.
